ess such questions by  giving him greater corporeal substance  placing him within a world of appetites  in a world of personal power  the goal is one I I know we all share  and that is to try to tell the truth  about our history thank you I'm happy to  take any questions if you have any yeah  um you seem to have moved somewhat in  your sense of Sally Hemings from your  earlier book the inner Jefferson to this  one what made you go back and you were  sort of a little skeptical of some of  the evidence faun Brody and so forth  what made you go back and what what if  eventually changed your mind into seeing  the the rationale for these arguments as  you say it was more than strictly DNA  where I presume right I had I had  problems with with both explanations and  I I do go into that in this book too  I felt that the Jefferson family denial  which persisted until the DNA evidence  which indicated which didn't indicate  but which emphasized that the Carr  brothers Jefferson's nephews frequented  the slave quarters and were the real  fathers one or the other of them were  both of them of Sally Hemings as  children there was anecdotal evidence to  support this but the Jefferson family  denial seemed until DNA as no less  credible than the African American oral  history which was passed down from the  slave community at Monticello and part  of the problem is that some of that oral  history has been disproved one of the  families that trace the lineage to  Jefferson and Sally Hemings has been  proven by DNA not to be biologically  linked but the cars have been  definitively excluded so DNA says that a  Jefferson fathered Sally hemings's  children or at least  Eston Hemings the last son born in 1808  Jefferson's last year as president I was  troubled what I wrote the inner  Jefferson that Jefferson's daughter  never explained to her children the  truth of Sally Hemings his conceptions  she grew up with Sally Hemings  there was no way that she didn't know  the truth why did sh